1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
Welcome to School Physics, Explained.

2
00:00:02,690 --> 00:00:09,406
This is AP Physics 1, Unit 1, Kinematics,
Part 2. In this video, we'll cover four

3
00:00:09,406 --> 00:00:09,986
topics.

4
00:00:10,436 --> 00:00:11,801
Free Fall Problems.

5
00:00:12,251 --> 00:00:13,766
Vectors and Components.

6
00:00:14,216 --> 00:00:15,517
Projectile Motion.

7
00:00:15,967 --> 00:00:17,226
Relative Motion.

8
00:00:17,676 --> 00:00:18,892
Let's get started.

9
00:00:20,533 --> 00:00:23,840
Free fall problems show up all over the A
P exam.

10
00:00:24,290 --> 00:00:29,369
They're just constant acceleration
problems, with the acceleration equal to

11
00:00:29,369 --> 00:00:30,114
negative g.

12
00:00:31,264 --> 00:00:33,717
Set up carefully. Choose up as positive.

13
00:00:34,167 --> 00:00:39,719
Then a is negative nine point eight meters
per second squared, for the whole flight:

14
00:00:39,719 --> 00:00:42,231
going up, at the top, and coming down.

15
00:00:43,381 --> 00:00:48,727
Example: a ball is thrown straight up from
the ground at fifteen meters per second.

16
00:00:48,727 --> 00:00:50,016
How high does it go?

17
00:00:50,466 --> 00:00:56,414
At the top, v is zero. Use v squared
equals v naught squared, plus two a delta

18
00:00:56,414 --> 00:00:56,567
y.

19
00:00:57,017 --> 00:01:03,392
Zero equals two hundred twenty five, minus
nineteen point six delta y. So the maximum

20
00:01:03,392 --> 00:01:06,468
height is about eleven point five meters.

21
00:01:07,618 --> 00:01:09,303
How long is the ball in the air?

22
00:01:09,753 --> 00:01:13,316
It lands back at its starting height, so
delta y is zero.

23
00:01:13,766 --> 00:01:20,411
Zero equals fifteen t, minus four point
nine t squared. So t is fifteen over four

24
00:01:20,411 --> 00:01:23,856
point nine, about three point one seconds.

25
00:01:25,006 --> 00:01:26,265
Notice the symmetry.

26
00:01:26,715 --> 00:01:31,643
The trip up takes as long as the trip
down, about one point five seconds each.

27
00:01:32,093 --> 00:01:36,830
And the ball lands with the same speed it
was thrown with, fifteen meters per

28
00:01:36,830 --> 00:01:38,429
second, but pointing down.

29
00:01:39,579 --> 00:01:40,880
Here's a common trap.

30
00:01:41,330 --> 00:01:47,190
At the top, the velocity is zero, but the
acceleration is not. It's still nine point

31
00:01:47,190 --> 00:01:50,120
eight meters per second squared, downward.

32
00:01:51,270 --> 00:01:52,742
Now try one yourself.

33
00:01:53,192 --> 00:01:58,804
A stone is dropped from a bridge, and hits
the water two point five seconds later.

34
00:01:58,804 --> 00:02:04,280
How high is the bridge, and how fast is
the stone moving at impact? Use g equals

35
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:06,333
ten meters per second squared.

36
00:02:06,783 --> 00:02:08,063
Pause the video now.

37
00:02:08,513 --> 00:02:13,935
The height is one half, times ten, times
two point five squared, which is about

38
00:02:13,935 --> 00:02:19,288
thirty one meters. The speed is ten times
two point five, which is twenty five

39
00:02:19,288 --> 00:02:20,524
meters per second.

40
00:02:20,824 --> 00:02:25,411
So far, everything moved along a line. Now
we go to two dimensions.

41
00:02:25,861 --> 00:02:29,871
To do that, we need one tool: breaking a
vector into components.

42
00:02:31,021 --> 00:02:34,861
A vector has a size and a direction. We
draw it as an arrow.

43
00:02:35,311 --> 00:02:39,948
Here's a velocity of ten meters per
second, at thirty degrees above the

44
00:02:39,948 --> 00:02:40,666
horizontal.

45
00:02:41,816 --> 00:02:47,433
Any vector can be replaced by two
perpendicular pieces: one along x, and one

46
00:02:47,433 --> 00:02:48,024
along y.

47
00:02:48,474 --> 00:02:52,549
Drop a line straight down from the tip.
That makes a right triangle.

48
00:02:52,999 --> 00:02:58,417
The horizontal side is the x component.
The vertical side is the y component.

49
00:02:59,567 --> 00:03:01,103
Use trig on the triangle.

50
00:03:01,553 --> 00:03:07,435
The x component is the magnitude times
cosine of the angle. The y component is

51
00:03:07,435 --> 00:03:10,300
the magnitude times sine of the angle.

52
00:03:10,750 --> 00:03:17,424
So v x is ten cosine thirty, about eight
point seven meters per second. And v y is

53
00:03:17,424 --> 00:03:21,331
ten sine thirty, exactly five meters per
second.

54
00:03:22,481 --> 00:03:26,492
You can also go backwards. Given the
components, find the vector.

55
00:03:26,942 --> 00:03:32,766
The magnitude comes from the Pythagorean
theorem: the square root of v x squared

56
00:03:32,766 --> 00:03:34,003
plus v y squared.

57
00:03:34,453 --> 00:03:38,485
And the angle is the inverse tangent of v
y over v x.

58
00:03:39,635 --> 00:03:41,875
Components make adding vectors easy.

59
00:03:42,325 --> 00:03:46,208
Just add the x parts together, and add the
y parts together.

60
00:03:46,658 --> 00:03:51,010
For example, three, four, plus one,
negative two, gives four, two.

61
00:03:52,160 --> 00:03:53,632
Now try one yourself.

62
00:03:54,082 --> 00:04:00,267
A force of twenty newtons points at sixty
degrees above the horizontal. Find its x

63
00:04:00,267 --> 00:04:01,549
and y components.

64
00:04:01,999 --> 00:04:03,279
Pause the video now.

65
00:04:03,729 --> 00:04:10,251
F x is twenty cosine sixty, which is ten
newtons. F y is twenty sine sixty, about

66
00:04:10,251 --> 00:04:12,667
seventeen point three newtons.

67
00:04:12,967 --> 00:04:15,740
Throw a ball sideways, and it follows a
curve.

68
00:04:16,190 --> 00:04:21,222
That curve looks complicated, but it's
really two simple motions happening at the

69
00:04:21,222 --> 00:04:21,844
same time.

70
00:04:22,994 --> 00:04:27,580
Here's the key idea. Horizontal and
vertical motion are independent.

71
00:04:28,030 --> 00:04:32,894
Watch two balls. One is dropped, and one
is launched sideways at the same moment.

72
00:04:33,344 --> 00:04:37,974
They hit the ground at the same time.
Gravity pulls both down the same way.

73
00:04:39,124 --> 00:04:40,553
So we split the motion.

74
00:04:41,003 --> 00:04:46,486
Horizontally, there is no acceleration, so
the velocity v x stays constant.

75
00:04:46,936 --> 00:04:51,373
Vertically, the acceleration is negative
g, just like free fall.

76
00:04:52,523 --> 00:04:57,930
Example: a ball rolls off a table one
point two meters high, at three meters per

77
00:04:57,930 --> 00:04:59,755
second. Where does it land?

78
00:05:00,205 --> 00:05:06,432
First, the vertical part gives the time.
One point two equals four point nine t

79
00:05:06,432 --> 00:05:10,530
squared, so t is about zero point four
nine seconds.

80
00:05:10,980 --> 00:05:16,845
Then the horizontal part gives the
distance. x equals three times zero point

81
00:05:16,845 --> 00:05:19,855
four nine, about one point five meters.

82
00:05:21,005 --> 00:05:25,549
Now a launch at an angle. Twenty meters
per second, at thirty degrees.

83
00:05:25,999 --> 00:05:31,947
Split the launch velocity. v x is about
seventeen point three, and v y is ten

84
00:05:31,947 --> 00:05:33,338
meters per second.

85
00:05:33,788 --> 00:05:39,677
At the peak, v y is zero, but v x is still
seventeen point three. The ball never

86
00:05:39,677 --> 00:05:41,297
stops moving sideways.

87
00:05:42,447 --> 00:05:48,220
For level ground, the time of flight is
two v y over g, about two point oh four

88
00:05:48,220 --> 00:05:48,804
seconds.

89
00:05:49,254 --> 00:05:54,865
So the range is seventeen point three
times two point oh four, about thirty five

90
00:05:54,865 --> 00:05:55,356
meters.

91
00:05:56,506 --> 00:05:57,978
Now try one yourself.

92
00:05:58,428 --> 00:06:03,796
A stone is thrown horizontally at eight
meters per second from a cliff twenty

93
00:06:03,796 --> 00:06:09,582
meters high. Use g equals ten meters per
second squared. How far from the base does

94
00:06:09,582 --> 00:06:10,140
it land?

95
00:06:10,590 --> 00:06:11,870
Pause the video now.

96
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:18,047
From twenty equals five t squared, t is
two seconds. Then x is eight times two,

97
00:06:18,047 --> 00:06:19,786
which is sixteen meters.

98
00:06:20,086 --> 00:06:24,673
You're on a train, and you toss a ball
straight up. It lands back in your hand.

99
00:06:25,123 --> 00:06:30,221
But someone on the platform sees the ball
fly forward in a curve. Who is right?

100
00:06:31,371 --> 00:06:35,672
Both are. Velocity depends on the
reference frame, the point of view you

101
00:06:35,672 --> 00:06:36,449
measure from.

102
00:06:36,899 --> 00:06:42,120
On the train, the ball moves only up and
down. From the platform, it also moves

103
00:06:42,120 --> 00:06:43,640
forward with the train.

104
00:06:44,790 --> 00:06:46,987
Here's the rule for adding velocities.

105
00:06:47,437 --> 00:06:53,379
The velocity of A relative to C equals the
velocity of A relative to B, plus the

106
00:06:53,379 --> 00:06:55,459
velocity of B relative to C.

107
00:06:55,909 --> 00:06:59,941
Read the subscripts like a chain: the
inner letters match and cancel.

108
00:07:01,091 --> 00:07:06,159
Example: you walk forward at one point
five meters per second inside a train

109
00:07:06,159 --> 00:07:08,493
moving at twenty meters per second.

110
00:07:08,943 --> 00:07:13,658
Relative to the ground, you move at twenty
one point five meters per second.

111
00:07:14,108 --> 00:07:19,000
Walk toward the back instead, and it's
twenty minus one point five, which is

112
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,447
eighteen point five meters per second.

113
00:07:22,597 --> 00:07:25,135
It works in two dimensions too, with
vectors.

114
00:07:25,585 --> 00:07:30,987
A boat heads straight across a river at
four meters per second. The current flows

115
00:07:30,987 --> 00:07:33,521
downstream at three meters per second.

116
00:07:33,971 --> 00:07:39,124
Add them as vectors. The speed relative to
the shore is the square root of four

117
00:07:39,124 --> 00:07:43,038
squared plus three squared, which is five
meters per second.

118
00:07:43,488 --> 00:07:48,987
The boat drifts downstream as it crosses,
at an angle of about thirty seven degrees

119
00:07:48,987 --> 00:07:50,379
from straight across.

120
00:07:51,529 --> 00:07:53,001
Now try one yourself.

121
00:07:53,451 --> 00:07:59,109
A plane flies north at two hundred forty
meters per second relative to the air. A

122
00:07:59,109 --> 00:08:04,697
wind blows east at seventy meters per
second. What is the plane's speed relative

123
00:08:04,697 --> 00:08:05,675
to the ground?

124
00:08:06,125 --> 00:08:07,405
Pause the video now.

125
00:08:07,855 --> 00:08:12,477
The square root of two hundred forty
squared plus seventy squared is two

126
00:08:12,477 --> 00:08:14,532
hundred fifty meters per second.

127
00:08:14,982 --> 00:08:20,401
That's the end of Unit 1. Next, we start
Unit 2: forces and Newton's laws.

128
00:08:21,551 --> 00:08:26,385
If this lesson helped, subscribe to School
Physics, Explained, and tap the bell so

129
00:08:26,385 --> 00:08:28,036
you don't miss the next one.

